You can delete and edit each graphic of a graphic group individually. To modify a graphic you can either
change it with the mouse in a 3D window or you can specify the new position, the rotation, the scaling,
or the material in the dialog Edit 3D Properties of the selected graphic.
Note
If you change any graphic or the graphic structure of a class object, this also changes all inherited
objects. If you change the graphics of a derived object, this only changes the object in which you
make the changes because 3D deactivates graphic inheritance if it has not been deactivated already.
As deactivating graphic inheritance causes graphics to be duplicated, we strongly advise you to make
changes in the class object as far as possible.
You can create, replace or delete the state graphic on the tab States in the dialog Edit 3D Properties
by selecting the orientation of the states for the state graphic: Horizontal, Vertical, or Off. When you
change this setting, 3D always deletes all state graphics and creates new state graphics afterward, which
are arranged according to your selection.
Back to Visualizing the Material Flow
Work with Graphics
Plant Simulation Help 10-987
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Inherit Graphics
Object graphics are, as other object properties, data of the object that can be inherited. This means
that objects, which inherit their graphics, share the graphics with their parent object and thus preserve
memory. Hence graphic changes in the class objects are automatically also visible in derived objects whose
graphic inheritance is activated.
Graphic changes in derived objects deactivate graphic inheritance. Then the derived object contains its
own, now changed, copy of the object graphic of the parent object and requires additional memory.
The following graphic changes deactivate graphic inheritance:
• Adding or deleting graphic groups or graphics.
• Transforming graphics, i. e., moving, rotating, zooming in or out of graphics.
• Changing the material of graphics.
• Grouping or ungrouping of a graphic group.
• Renaming of a graphic group.
• Changing the graphic group settings internal or locked.
Note
As turning graphic inheritance off makes your model file larger and slower from a certain point on, we
strongly advise you to make graphic changes in the class object as far as possible.
You can activate or deactivate graphic inheritance as follows:
• By clicking the inheritance check box under Home > Edit 3D Properties > Graphics > Graphic Groups
so that it looks like this 
 .
Inherit Graphics
10-988 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• By clicking Inherit Graphic Groups 
  in the dialog Show Graphic Structure.
When you turn graphic inheritance on, the object will use the graphics of its parent again and cancel your
changes.
When you leave graphic inheritance off, the object keeps its changed graphics of the instance and thus
uses more memory.
See also
_3D.InheritGraphics [SimTalk]
Inherit Graphics
