Use a Different Graphic for an Object
In some cases it will suffice to model the machines of your plant with the default graphics we provide for
the material flow objects and for the mobile objects. In other cases you will want to replace these default
graphics with pictures that resemble the actual machines used in your plant. In some cases you might even
have the actual graphics files of these machines from the builder of the machine, which you can then use.
Note
If you change any graphic or the graphic structure of a class object, this also changes all inherited
objects. If you change the graphics of a derived object, this only changes the object in which you make
the changes because Plant Simulation deactivates off graphic inheritance if it has not been deactivated
already. As deactivating graphic inheritance causes graphics to be duplicated, we strongly advise you to
make changes in the class object as far as possible.
We saved the graphics that are part of your program package in s3d format, we already defined animation
paths for most of them, and we scaled the objects correctly, so that they match the size of the other
objects.
Use a Different Graphic for an Object
Plant Simulation Help 10-993
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Instead, you can also select a graphic which you created in 3D with the command Export Scene > Export
Object.
To show an object with a different graphic, you can do one of the following:
• Exchange the object graphic with another pre-defined object graphic
Select the object, and click 
  Exchange Graphics on the Edit ribbon tab of the 3D window or select
the command on the context menu.
Plant Simulation opens the dialog Exchange Graphics. By default it opens to the folder where
you installed Plant Simulation, namely C:\Program Files\Siemens\Tecnomatix Plant Simulation
XX\3D\s3D-graphics. Change to the folder Plant Simulation\3D\s3d-graphics and select one of the
s3d files. You might have to open one of the folders within that folder.
Click Open to replace all graphic and animation data of the selected object with data from the
selected .s3d-file. This means that all graphic groups, the state graphic, and the animation attributes
will be removed from the selected object and graphics and animation attributes of the saved object will
be added instead.
Select the .s3D graphic that you want to use.
Note
Make sure that Preview 
  is selected, so that you can see what the graphic looks like.
To exchange graphics, you can also drag an .s3d-file from a File Explorer window and drop it onto a 3D
window.
Use a Different Graphic for an Object
10-994 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Insert the graphic of an existing graphics file
Select the graphic, and click 
  Graphics on the Edit ribbon tab of 3D.
Plant Simulation opens the dialog Open. By default it opens to the folder where you installed Plant
Simulation, namely C:\Program Files\Siemens\Tecnomatix Plant Simulation XX\3D\jt-graphics.
Select the .jt graphic that you want to use.
Instead, you can also import a graphic which you created in 3D with the command Export Scene
> Export Graphics. Or you can import a graphics file that you exported from another 3D modeling
program in a format that Plant Simulation can import.
After Plant Simulation inserted the imported graphics, it will add them to the selected graphic group
while keeping any already existing graphics. Delete any graphics that you do not need any longer.
To import graphics you can also drag a file of a supported graphic format from a File Explorer window
and drop it onto a 3D window.
• Create your own object graphic
Open the object, click 
  Insert Shape on the Edit ribbon tab of 3D
and select the shape that you want to create.
After Plant Simulation inserted the shape you define, it will add it to the selected graphic group while
keeping any already existing graphics. Delete any graphics that you do not need any longer.
You can also use graphics from other graphic groups by opening the dialog Show Graphic Structure
[context menu] of the source object, copying the desired graphic with the respective context menu
command, and then inserting it into the destination object with Ctrl+V.
• Create a complex graphic by inserting multiple shapes or imported graphics and position, rotate, scale,
or color them.
If you are working with a simulation model, which you created in a previous version, you can use the
icons of the 2D objects and map them to a Textured Plate in 3D.
Use a Different Graphic for an Object
