Creating Your Own Objects
Create Your Own Objects
You can create your own graphics to meet your modeling needs in your simulation mode.
Proceed as follows:
• Duplicate or derive any of the class objects that we provide in Class Library and that have similar
properties as the one you would like to create.
You can then edit the 3D properties of this new class object, so that it fits your needs, for example by
using another graphic (installation folder > 3D > s3d graphics), work with animation paths, etc.
• Import a three-dimensional graphic from the Plant Simulation graphics library that is part of your
program package (installation folder > 3D > jt graphics), from your own graphics library, or from a
graphics program that can save files in a format that Plant Simulation can import, into your simulation
model. After you imported a graphic that you downloaded from the internet or created in a 3D modeling
program, you might have to scale, rotate and move it to the origin of the scene.
You can then transform the graphic into a simulation object. To do so, click the graphic with the right
mouse button and select Make Simulation Object. If the thus created simulation object can contain
additional simulation objects, you might also want to adjust its animation paths to the changed object
graphic, compare Work with Animation Paths.
• Model your own 3D graphic by inserting a shape as often as required, position, rotate, scale or color
it. You can also use any graphics from other graphic groups and edit them. If you want to use several
graphics for your object graphic, you have to group these individual graphics to be able to transform
the group into a simulation object later on. Click the graphic with the right mouse button and select
Make Simulation Object. In case the thus created simulation object can include additional simulation
objects, you should also adjust its simulation paths to the changed object graphic, compare Working
with Animation Paths.
• Create a simulation object with animation to make your simulation model look as close as possible as
it its in reality.
Video on YouTube
