Graphic Groups [described]
Using Alternative Graphic Groups
Use Alternative Graphic Groups
In this sample model we show how to model a simplified airplane and how to show the progress of the
airplane assembly. To do so, we show the selected graphic groups which graphically display the finished
components.
We will solve the following modeling tasks:
• Create a New MU Class for the Object
• Create Alternative Graphic Groups for the Components
• Show the Progress of the Airplane Assembly
Back to Work with Graphics
Create a New MU Class for the Object
We first create a new MU class for the object for our alternative graphic group.
First, we will create a new MU class that represents the airplane we are going to build:
Using Alternative Graphic Groups
Plant Simulation Help 10-1017
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Open the folder MUs in the Class Library.
• Click the object Container with the right mouse button and select Duplicate.
• Press F2 and rename the newly created Container to Airplane.
While you’re at it, you can also rename the Frame named Models in the folder Models to
MyAirplaneAssembly.
Back to Work with Graphics
Create Alternative Graphic Groups for the Components
After creating the MU class Airplane for our model, we will create the graphics of the different components
of the airplane. For demonstration purposes we will content ourselves with the Shapes that Plant
Simulation provides. In a real situation, we would naturally use the CAD drawings we received from the
engineering department.
• Open the Frame containing our simulation model, My3DAirplaneAssembly in our case.
• Edit the MU class Airplane. Click it in the Class Library with the right mouse button and select Open
in 3D. Click into the background of the Frame .UserObjects.Airplane with the right mouse button and
select Edit 3D Properties.
Create Alternative Graphic Groups for the Components
10-1018 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Note
As we do not need the default graphic of our airplane, the pallet with the name default, we delete
this graphic by selecting it and pressing Del.
• Click Add to add a new graphic group. We first want to add the graphic group for the fuselage of the
airplane. Type Fuselage into the text box Name.
Once you clicked OK in the dialog above and Apply in the dialog Edit 3D Properties, the dialog Show
Graphic Structure shows our new graphic group as well.
Create Alternative Graphic Groups for the Components
Plant Simulation Help 10-1019
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Now we create the actual graphic that represents the fuselage. Change to the Edit ribbon tab and
select Cylinder.
• Select the graphic group to which you want to add the newly created graphic, Fuselage in our case.
Enter the dimension of the cylinder and click Create.
Plant Simulation attaches the cylinder to the cursor in the 3D window .UserObjects.Airplane. Drag it to
the position of your choice and click the left mouse button to insert it.
Note
Make sure that Scale automatically on the tab Transformation is cleared, otherwise Plant Simulation
will not show the cylinder with the settings you entered.
Create Alternative Graphic Groups for the Components
10-1020 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• 3D inserts the cylinder in the upright position. This is not what we want though. We need our fuselage
to be in the horizontal position. To rotate it by 90 degrees, click it with the right mouse button and
select Edit 3D Properties.
• Enter the settings from the screenshot below and click Apply to rotate the fuselage by an angle of 90
degrees on the y-axis.
Create Alternative Graphic Groups for the Components
Plant Simulation Help 10-1021
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• In the next step, we are going to add the graphic group for the cockpit of our airplane. Click with the
right mouse button in the Frame .UserObjects.Airplane and select Edit 3D Properties. Change to the
tab Graphics and click Add. Type Cockpit into the text box Name and click OK in the dialog. Then click
Apply in the dialog Edit 3D Properties.
• Then, create the actual graphic that represents the cockpit. Change to the Edit ribbon tab and select
Cone Frustum 
 .
Select the graphic group to which you want to add the newly created graphic, Cockpit in our case. Enter
the dimension of the cone and click Create. Plant Simulation attaches the cone to the cursor in the 3D
window .UserObjects.Airplane. Drag it to the position of your choice and click the left mouse button to
insert it.
• Rotate the Cockpit by 90 degrees and place it at the right-hand side of the fuselage. We used the
settings shown the screenshot below.
Create Alternative Graphic Groups for the Components
