Displaying the Progress of the Airplane Assembly
Show the Progress of the Airplane Assembly
In our last step we are going to show the progress of the airplane assembly in our plant by successively
hiding and showing graphic groups as the airplane moves from processing station to processing station.
We will demonstrate how to:
• Insert and Configure the Source and the Processing Stations
• Program the Visibility Controls
• Change Orientation and Position of the Object on the Stations
Back to Work with Graphics
Insert and Configure the Source and the Processing Stations
First, we are going to insert the Source that produces the airplane and the stations that process and
assemble it. Connect the stations with Connectors.
Displaying the Progress of the Airplane Assembly
10-1028 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

While you are at it, you might also insert a Method below each of the stations and name them as shown in
the screenshot. We will then program the individual methods in the next step.
Our finished model looks like this:
• Insert and configure the Source so that it matches the screenshots below. Make sure that it produces a
MU of type Airplane. Select the method hidePlane as the Exit Control on the tab Controls.
Insert and Configure the Source and the Processing Stations
Plant Simulation Help 10-1029
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Insert and configure the Station. Enter a Processing Time of 10 seconds (0:10). Select the method
showFuselage as the Entrance Control on the tab Controls.
• Insert and configure next station, called Station1. Enter a Processing Time of 10 seconds. Select the
method showTailAssembly as the Entrance Control on the tab Controls.
• Insert and configure final station, called Station2. Enter a Processing Time of 10 seconds. Select the
method showWings as the Entrance Control on the tab Controls.
Back to Show the Progress of the Airplane Assembly
Program the Visibility Controls
Next, we are going to program the methods that we use as Exit Control and as Entrance Control of the
stations for hiding and showing graphic groups as the airplane moves from station to station.
To do so, we will use the attribute Visible.
• Program the method hidePlane, which we use as the Exit Control of the Source. It hides all
components of the airplane. Once all components are hidden, it moves the airplane on to the next
station.
We entered the following source code:
@._3D.getGraphic("Fuselage").Visible := false
@._3D.getGraphic("Cockpit").Visible := false
@._3D.getGraphic("LeftWing").Visible := false
@._3D.getGraphic("RightWing").Visible := false
@._3D.getGraphic("TailAssembly").Visible := false
@.move -- move the airplane on to the next station
• Program the method showFuselage, which we use as the Entrance Control of the station named
Station. It shows the fuselage and the cockpit of the airplane.
Program the Visibility Controls
10-1030 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

We entered the following source code:
@._3D.getGraphic("Fuselage").Visible := true
@._3D.getGraphic("Cockpit").Visible := true
• Program the method showTailAssembly, which we use as the Entrance Control of the station named
Station1. It shows the tail assembly of the airplane.
We entered the following source code:
@._3D.getGraphic("TailAssembly").Visible := true
• Program the method showWings, which we use as the Entrance Control of the station named Station2.
It shows the wings of the airplane.
We entered the following source code:
@._3D.getGraphic("LeftWing").Visible := true
@._3D.getGraphic("RightWing").Visible := true
• As we are running the simulation, we want to see a smooth animation. A Real-time factor of 16 in the
dialog of the EventController in our simulation model ensures this.
• Let’s run the simulation to check if everything works as intended, which indeed it does.
Program the Visibility Controls
Plant Simulation Help 10-1031
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Back to Show the Progress of the Airplane Assembly
Change Orientation and Position of the Object on the Stations
You can adjust the orientation and the position the airplane on the stations. The default orientation of the
airplane looks realistic on a conveyor, modeled for example with a Conveyor.
It does not look good on the Stations that we used though.
We are going to changes this:
• First, let’s rotate the airplane by 90 degrees to the right. To do so, click the class of the airplane with
the right mouse button, and select Open in 3D. Click the right mouse button in the background of the
window and select Edit 3D Properties. Enter 90 into the text box Angle on the tab Transformation >
Rotation.
Change Orientation and Position of the Object on the Stations
10-1032 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Run the simulation again to show the result of the rotation.
• Now let’s change the position of the airplane on the stations. This is necessary, because by default Plant
Simulation places the MUs at the origin of its scene, i.e., the point where the two red lines intersect.
This is why the tail of our airplane is placed on the reference point of the stations.
To change the position of the airplane on the stations, open the class of the airplane in a new window.
Press Ctrl+A to select all graphic groups of the airplane. Move them to the left with the left arrow key
until the center of the wings is located on the y-axis. We also move the plane up on the z-axis by holding
down Ctrl and Shift and pressing the up arrow key.
Change Orientation and Position of the Object on the Stations
Plant Simulation Help 10-1033
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Run the simulation again to show the result of centering the airplane on the processing stations.
Back to Show the Progress of the Airplane Assembly
Displaying Object States
Show Object States
You can show the states of the objects in your simulation model with State graphics.
State graphics show the States of the Material Flow Objects. A state graphic can show several states at
the same time.
You can select the settings for the state graphic on the Tab States in the dialog Edit 3D Properties.
Displaying Object States
10-1034 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

We will demonstrate how to:
• Show States on a Signal Post Above the Machine
• Show States at the Front of the Machine
Back to Work with Graphics
Show States on a Signal Post Above the Machine
When you select to show vertically arranged state graphics for a machine for which you imported
or created a graphic, you will have to adjust the position settings of the state graphics so that Plant
Simulation show them correctly without overlapping a part of the machine.
By default, vertical states are shown with a signal lamp on a gray signal post positioned at the left top rear
corner of the standard graphics. This way you don’t have to visualize the states to be able to move them
around without the dialog Edit 3D Properties.
To activate vertically arranged state graphics, open the object in 3D, click the right mouse button into
the background of the 3D window, and select Edit 3D Properties.
Show States on a Signal Post Above the Machine
Plant Simulation Help 10-1035
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Our default graphic of the Station with vertically arranged state graphics on a signal post looks like this:
Back to Show Object States
Show States at the Front of the Machine
Plant Simulation show the states as horizontally arranged cubes at the back of the graphic of the
machine by default.
Show States at the Front of the Machine
10-1036 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Depending on the look of the graphic of the machine this may not be practical as the graphic of the
machine might hide the state graphics as such. To fix this, we’ll demonstrate how to show the state
graphics at the front of the machine.
Our sample graphic without state graphics looks like this:
To activate horizontally arranged cubes, open the object in 3D, click the right mouse button into the
background of the 3D window, and select Edit 3D Properties.
Show States at the Front of the Machine
Plant Simulation Help 10-1037
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• To show the state graphics, click Show All Possible States. Rotate the machine to show the state
graphics. You’ll notice that the default setting is not helpful as all state graphics are hidden by the
machine when we look at it from the front and as some of them disappear inside of the machine.
• To fix that, let’s move the horizontal states to the front of the machine. You can either
◦ Move the state graphics with the arrow keys. Select the state graphics bar. Press the Up or Down
arrow to move it in the Y direction. Press the Left or Right arrow to move it in the X direction. Hold
down Ctrl and press the Up or Down arrow to move it in the Z direction. Or you can
◦ Move the state graphics with the mouse. Select the state graphics bar. Drag the mouse left and
right to move it in the Y direction. Drag the mouse back and forth to move it in the X direction. Hold
down the Ctrl key and drag the mouse up and down to move it in the Z direction.
You’ll notice that moving objects with the arrow keys and/or the mouse might not be accurate
enough. Instead, you can also enter precise values into the dialog Edit 3D Properties. If you want
to, you can also combine the three methods by first moving the object with the arrow keys, or the
mouse, to roughly position the object where you want it to be and by then entering precise values
into the dialog.
◦ Click the machine with the right mouse button and select Show Graphic Structure. Then select Edit
3D Properties in the window Show Graphic Structure.
◦ Change the position on the tab Transformation so that X reads -1.07, so that Y reads -0.2, and
that Z reads 0.25.
◦ Click Apply to check if the result looks like we expected it to be. The state graphics bar moved to the
front of the machine, but it is too high. To fix that, we scale it in the Z direction. Clear the check box
Uniform and enter 0.9 into the text box Z. Now it looks good.
